# docs/LIBERTIES.md IS NOT MERGED WITH `union`, AND THE REASON IS MEASURED.
#
# The liberty ledger is the same shape of artifact as the changelog — a numbered
# register every branch adds an entry to — and the root .gitattributes already
# carries the changelog's version of this note. This is the ledger's, written
# where the file it governs lives.
#
# T-0186 asked for "`.gitattributes` treatment so the merge itself stops being
# silent", after two branches each took L177 on 2026-08-24, merged clean, and
# committed a ledger with one number on two entries. The measurement below is
# why the treatment is NO RULE, and why the weight sits on the gate instead.
#
# Measured 2026-08-27 on a scratch clone of the committed 8,787-line file, two
# branches off one base, default merge driver:
#
#   both branches APPEND AT THE END OF THE FILE   ->  CONFLICT. Loud, and safe.
#   one appends at EOF, one inserts mid-file      ->  CLEAN. Two `### L198`
#                                                     headings, git says nothing.
#
# The first case is the operation `docs/LIBERTIES.md` mandates — "a new liberty
# is appended at the END OF THIS FILE" — and it already stops you. That is not
# an accident of this file; it is what a text merge does when two sides insert
# after the same last line. The second case is the 2026-08-24 incident exactly,
# and it was reachable then because `## Resolved` was still the LAST section, so
# "append at the end of the file" and "append at the end of the register" were
# two different places. T-0054 moved Resolved above the register, which is what
# collapsed the everyday collision into the loud case.
#
# So `union` would not have saved that merge. It would have LOST the one the
# default driver already catches: union keeps both insertions instead of raising
# a conflict, which turns the mandated append from a conflict into a silently
# duplicated number. That is the same trade the changelog made and paid for —
# five silent corruptions in one day, 2026-08-15, each needing the hand repair
# the conflict would have demanded up front. Reaching for union here would buy
# the identical failure in a register whose entire job is to be trustworthy.
#
# What remains is the second case, which no merge driver can see: two additions
# in different parts of a long file, neither touching the other's lines. That is
# the gate's to catch, and since T-0186 it does — `tools/compile_liberties.py`
# reports a number taken twice with both titles, and reports a heading that
# looks like an entry and does not parse, which is the same silence one step
# earlier. `tools/check.sh` fails on either. Both are asserted in the tool's own
# self-test, on a specimen carrying the fault and on the committed document
# beside it, so neither check can quietly stop working.
#
# A conflict is safe because it stops you. A clean merge of two appends to a
# numbered ledger is not, and the check is what stands in for the conflict.
#
# No rules are set in this file on purpose. If you add one, measure it first.
